        answer: 1,
        why_en: "It is an absence of two enzymes, not an energetic barrier -- the seed performs the same conversion on the same acetyl-CoA every spring. “ isocitrate lyase and malate synthase are unique to the glyoxylate cycle ” (A p.736), and with them the cycle bypasses both decarboxylations so “ two acetyl groups enter the cycle and four carbons leave as succinate ” . Animals have neither enzyme and, because pyruvate dehydrogenase is effectively irreversible, no way back to pyruvate either, so “ animals cannot bring about the net synthesis of glucose from fatty acids ” . Option 1 misdiagnoses the obstacle. Option 3 is false about both organisms. Option 4 invents a human glyoxylate cycle. Two riders keep the answer honest: the word NET matters, since fatty-acid carbons do enter glucose by exchange without net gain, and the glycerol backbone of a triacylglycerol IS glucogenic in animals.",
        why_cn: "**这是「缺两个酶」，不是「能量上的障碍」**——那粒种子每年春天都在用同样的 acetyl-CoA 做同一个转化。**「isocitrate lyase 与 malate synthase 是乙醛酸循环独有的」**（A 第 736 页），有了它们，循环就绕开了两步脱羧，于是**「两个乙酰基进入循环，四个碳作为琥珀酸离开」**。动物**两个酶都没有**；又因为**丙酮酸脱氢酶实际上不可逆**，**回丙酮酸这条路也堵死了**，所以**「动物不能实现由脂肪酸净合成葡萄糖」**。**选项 1** 诊断错了障碍所在。**选项 3** 关于两种生物都说错了。**选项 4** 凭空发明了一个人类乙醛酸循环。**两条补充让答案保持诚实**：**「净」这个词很要紧**（脂肪酸的碳确实能靠交换进入葡萄糖，但没有净增加），而且**三酰甘油的甘油骨架在动物体内也是生糖的**。"
